+# Shot memory audit — October 31, 2026
+
+Historical baseline before the runtime reuse changes: see `runtime-allocations.md`
+and `precomputed-shots.md` for the current solveInto API and fixed-size characterization.
+
+## Version and limits
+
+Audited the working tree based on 9278df5 and the older calculator implementations
+in 2933557, be9839d, and c59b459. No live roboRIO, crash message, heap dump, kernel
+OOM report, or deployed build identity was available. The user's observation that
+pre-calculator code works is evidence of a regression, but does not isolate the
+calculator from other changes introduced with it. This audit cannot certify every
+allocation or leak in native vendor libraries.
+
+## Current runtime computation
+
+RobotContainer loads one PrecomputedShots model from the deploy CSV. The only
+ordinary constructor that integrates physics is called by the offline generator
+and software tests. There is no automatic runtime fallback to physics.
+
+- Startup parses 561 CSV flights at 5 RPM spacing. Reader closes via try-with-resources.
+  Row strings, split arrays, numeric-parser temporaries and signature reflection
+  objects become eligible for collection. The model retains the table.
+- The model also retains a 281-element boxed RPM list and 281 flight references,
+  pointing into the same table rather than duplicating those Flight objects.
+  Its six scratch arrays are fixed size. Estimated table/list storage is 47,144
+  bytes with the existing approximate sizing method; this is not a heap-dump measurement.
+- Dashboard solve runs at 2 Hz. Shot-command solve runs when range changes by more
+  than 2 cm, limited to 10 Hz. Each scans 281 entries and returns a Shot record,
+  replacing the previous diagnostic record. There is no solve-history collection.
+- Release readiness runs each command iteration. scores() uses neighboring table
+  entries for measured RPM. No integrations or temporary Flight objects.
+- The coefficient setter is called each iteration, but unchanged coefficient 1.025
+  returns immediately. A different coefficient on a table model is rejected; it
+  does not trigger simulations. The offline numerical model clears/replaces its
+  bounded cache when its coefficient changes.
+
+## Measured allocation test
+
+ShotMemoryAuditTest warms up 10,000 solves, then runs 100,000 solves and 100,000
+readiness checks on the saved model. The desktop JVM's per-thread allocation
+counter reported:
+
+- solve: 9,600,000 bytes total, 96 bytes per call (Shot plus diagnostics).
+- scores: zero bytes during the measured loop.
+- physics integrations: zero.
+- cache count and estimated bytes: unchanged.
+
+The result objects are deliberately allowed to escape to avoid benchmarking only
+an optimized-away result. At the current combined maximum 12 solves/sec, this
+corresponds to about 1,152 bytes/sec for these records, excluding all callers and
+framework work. JVM object sizes/optimizations can differ on the roboRIO. This is
+an allocation measurement, not a full retained-heap or native-memory profile.
+
+## Historical calculator pressure
+
+The earlier calculator did run integrations at startup, while solving, and for
+measured-RPM scoring. Each flight allocated six double arrays plus launch/result
+objects. A successful solve in 2933557 integrated the midpoint twice, once directly
+and once through scores(). Earlier versions also refined band edges through
+repeated integrations; moving-shot code had further seven-element RK4 arrays.
+These are temporary allocations and CPU work, not evidence of an unbounded leak.
+They can still increase GC pauses and worsen a controller already near its limit.
+No full state history was retained for each integration timestep.
+
+## Other memory paths found in the shooting feature
+
+1. Geometry/aiming: releasePosition constructs immutable translations; aim uses
+   additional translations, chassis-speed objects and profiled-controller states.
+   Temporary allocation continues even with a precomputed shot table.
+2. Diagnostics: numeric boxing and some status strings are constructed before
+   telemetry's limiter. Cached latest values keep the most recent object for each
+   key; superseded objects are eligible for collection. Fixed-key use is bounded.
+3. Logging: the latest-value caches each cap at 2,048 keys. This bounds key count,
+   not the size of an arbitrary array/string value. AdvantageKit also retains its
+   current log table and a bounded receiver backlog. Hardware frame admission now
+   runs at 2 Hz; a slow receiver can still retain up to its 500-frame queue capacity.
+   A bounded queue can exhaust a small heap without being an infinite leak.
+4. Characterization: velocitySamples and voltageSamples append for as long as the
+   optional feedforward characterization command runs. They are cleared at its next
+   initialization, but retained by the dashboard command afterward. This is the
+   clear unbounded-duration collection found in the shooter subsystem; normal shots
+   do not run it.
+5. Commands/controllers: model and command objects are retained for reuse. No
+   command creation or button-binding registration was found inside the normal shot
+   execution loop. WPILib 2026.2.1 caches controller trigger objects; repeated calls
+   to rightTrigger() may allocate lookup keys but do not register a new trigger each time.
+6. Hardware and vision: CTRE motor/CAN objects and NetworkTables use native memory;
+   vision creates pose/tag arrays every update. Their allocations and driver state
+   are outside the measured model loop. The flywheel visualizer/physics simulator
+   is desktop-only and is not the real robot's shot predictor.
+
+## Garbage collection and crash diagnosis
+
+Java collects unreachable Java objects automatically. It cannot free a referenced
+cache, retained command history, or native allocations. It can reuse freed heap
+space without returning the committed heap pages to Linux. A Linux OOM kill can
+therefore happen even with a modest live Java heap. Adding System.gc() to the robot
+loop is not a fix and could cause control pauses; no explicit GC was added.
+
+Collect the exact failure message and these readings on the failing deployed build:
+HeapUsedMiB, HeapCommittedMiB, HeapMaxMiB, ProcessResidentMiB, SystemAvailableMiB,
+and Logger/QueuedCycles. Heap growth suggests Java retention; stable heap with
+process growth suggests native memory or JVM overhead; system-wide pressure can
+also come from other processes. Distinguish Java OutOfMemoryError, Linux OOM kill,
+Watchdog overruns, brownout, and ordinary exceptions instead of inferring the cause
+from a free-memory number alone.
+
+The audit found and corrected an escaped-regex bug in MemoryMonitor: valid Linux
+memory lines could fail parsing and be silently ignored. Its parser now has tests.
+This diagnostic fix and the audit tests are local changes, not deployed evidence.
diff --git a/docs/telemetry-rate-limit.md b/docs/telemetry-rate-limit.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..9880449
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/telemetry-rate-limit.md
@@ -0,0 +1,51 @@
+# Two-hertz telemetry
+
+Application log writes and input serialization go through `RateLimitedTelemetry`.
+Each key may be written at most once per 500,000 microseconds. Intermediate
+updates replace one pending value; `Robot.robotPeriodic()` flushes pending values,
+including a final command stop after the command is no longer running. The three
+caches (outputs, inputs, dashboard) each have a fixed maximum of 2,048 keys and do
+not retain a history of suppressed samples.
+
+All AdvantageKit receivers, including WPILOGWriter and NT4Publisher, are wrapped
+in `RateLimitedReceiver`. Entire frames are forwarded at most once per 500,000
+microseconds, so AdvantageKit's automatic DriverStation, Console, SystemStats,
+performance, and other fields are also recorded/transmitted at no more than 2 Hz.
+This cap uses the frame's robot timestamp, including during accelerated replay.
+
+On hardware, scalar dashboard diagnostics use the same per-key cap. Desktop
+simulation retains immediate dashboard state for control tests and interactive
+simulation, but its recorded log frames and application log writes are still capped.
+Chooser/command sendables, operator tuning requests, camera-control NetworkTables
+messages, and Driver Station errors are control/error interfaces, not sampled
+telemetry, and are not delayed by this limiter.
+
+Removed logging work:
+
+- Indexer mechanism animation and Flywheel/Mechanism serialization.
+- Repeated LED color strings (the physical LED animation still runs normally).
+- Bulk BLine trajectory/pose graphics and the duplicate Fuel hub/release poses.
+- YAGSL's independent telemetry and the legacy Swerve/Field dashboard graphic.
+- Duplicate scalar swerve pose dashboard values; use the sampled Swerve/Pose log.
+
+Swerve raw/adjusted absolute encoder dashboard updates and encoder read-error
+telemetry are disabled. Encoder feedback remains active for drivetrain control.
+
+Sensors, scheduler execution, vision fusion, shooting readiness checks, motor
+commands and physical LED updates retain their normal control-loop frequency.
+Replay still consumes logged inputs every replay cycle. A sparse 2 Hz log cannot
+reproduce the original 50 Hz control decisions with full fidelity.
+
+On hardware, `SampledLoggedRobot` runs AdvantageKit's before/after capture hooks
+only once per 500,000 microseconds, before table cloning and queue admission.
+The scheduler and all control callbacks still execute every 20 ms. Both WPILOG
+and NT4 receivers remain enabled. Desktop simulation/replay retain every-cycle
+capture. Startup and exception frames are additional diagnostic exceptions.
+
+The framework is adapted from AdvantageKit 26.0.2's BSD-licensed LoggedRobot in
+the same package to access package-private capture hooks. Review this adaptation
+when upgrading AdvantageKit. This reduces queue admission from 50 to 2 frames/s;
+it does not establish that logging caused the memory exhaustion.
+
+Brief faults between samples, including final console output before a crash, may be
+absent from the sampled log; retain the Driver Station console when diagnosing a failure.
diff --git a/docs/vision-testing.md b/docs/vision-testing.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..71a6d79
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/vision-testing.md
@@ -0,0 +1,116 @@
+# Testing camera position and shooting readiness
+
+The saved AdvantageKit `.wpilog` now contains the vision diagnostics. Open it in
+AdvantageScope, or connect AdvantageScope to the robot live. Look under RealOutputs
+in a saved log (or AdvantageKit/RealOutputs live). Start with these folders:
+
+- `Vision/Front`: physical front camera, network name `limelight-front`.
+- `Vision/Left`: physical left camera, network name `limelight-left`.
+- `Vision/Right`: physical right camera, network name `limelight-right`.
+- `Swerve/Pose`: the combined robot position and heading used for driving/aiming.
+- `Fuel/Status`: first reason feeding is blocked. `Fuel/Readiness` shows individual
+  conditions; `Fuel/Feeding` is the actual automatic feed decision.
+
+## A short test
+
+1. Release automatic aim. With the robot stationary and a tag visible, check the
+   physical camera's `Connected`, `TargetVisible`, `PrimaryTagID`, and `TagCount`.
+   `TagIDs` is the full list contributing to the MegaTag2 estimate when supplied
+   by the camera. The primary target can be a different tag from that full list.
+2. Check `ConfiguredMount` against `ReportedMount`. The configured Pose3d is a
+   container for Limelight forward/right/up values, NOT a WPILib left-positive
+   transform. ReportedMount is [forward, right, up, roll, pitch, yaw] in meters/degrees.
+   Code writes mounting configuration at startup, overriding previous web settings.
+3. Put `Swerve/Pose`, `Vision/Front/MegaTag1Pose`, and
+   `Vision/Front/MegaTag2Pose` on the same 2D field. Camera poses are arrays with
+   one robot pose, or an empty array when no tagged finite estimate is available.
+   A missing reading is not drawn as a robot at (0,0). Check frame ages too:
+   even a finite tagged pose may be stale. Camera poses show estimates, including
+   rejected ones; only Swerve/Pose is the combined result used for driving.
+4. Rotate gently without translating. Check Swerve/Pose rotation and
+   `Vision/Front/HeadingSentDeg`. Both should track the actual robot rotation.
+   MegaTag2 uses the heading supplied by the robot; it is not an independent
+   check that the robot's direction sensor is correct. Normal vision fusion
+   effectively ignores camera heading corrections.
+5. Read `Status`. `Used` means a new reading was inserted this loop; `Waiting for
+   new frame` between camera frames is normal. Use `SecondsSinceUsed` to judge
+   whether updates continue. -1 means none has been used since program startup.
+   Disconnected cameras, absent tags, invalid data, timestamps more than 0.5 s
+   old, repeated frames, rapid rotation, and >2 m position differences are rejected.
+6. If the camera pose matches the real robot but Swerve/Pose is >2 m away, the
+   jump guard prevents automatic recovery. The current driver X button explicitly
+   uses the first camera with a fresh tagged MegaTag1 pose (left, front, then
+   right), including its AprilTag-derived heading. First verify that pose while
+   stationary, then use the
+   reset in a controlled teleop test. `Vision/Front/ResetStatus` records the result.
+   Do not use a reset to hide a wrong field map or a jumping camera estimate.
+7. Test shooting only after the combined pose is believable. Hold the aimed-shot
+   control and read Fuel/Status, Fuel/Readiness, Fuel/RangeMeters, and HubAim logs.
+   "Settling" is the short required readiness dwell. "Ready" and Feeding=true
+   should command nonzero Indexer/FloorTargetRPM and Indexer/TunnelTargetRPM.
+   Nonzero targets with no physical motion indicate an output/hardware issue;
+   zero targets with Feeding=false indicate a release condition has not passed.
+   Readiness booleans describe the last active command execution; Fuel/Status
+   "Released" and Feeding=false identify an inactive command.
+
+Driver Start sets heading to 0 degrees on blue or 180 degrees on red; an unknown
+alliance skips the reset. In the blue-origin field
+frame, 0 means facing the red alliance wall, not facing whichever tag is visible.
+
+## A single practice tag is a miniature field-map test
+
+Tag numbers identify fixed field landmarks, not robot cameras. Any camera can
+see any tag. In WPILib's official 2026 welded field map, tag 25 is on the blue
+hub's face toward the blue wall, at approximately X=4.02186 m, Y=4.39024 m,
+Z=1.12395 m with yaw=180 degrees. Its center is about 44.25 inches above the
+floor. Tag 26 is beside it on the same face.
+
+If a loose tag 25 is placed somewhere else, the camera STILL assumes it occupies
+that mapped location and orientation. Either reproduce the mapped arrangement
+(including tag size, height and upright orientation), interpreting room positions
+as a virtual competition field, or upload a measured practice map to each camera.
+A practice map used with hub auto-aim must share the robot code's field coordinate
+frame and hub location. One tag can support localization, but viewing multiple
+correctly placed tags generally gives a stronger geometric constraint. A tag
+number alone does not tell the camera where the actual practice hub is.
+
+Official map:
+https://raw.githubusercontent.com/wpilibsuite/allwpilib/v2026.2.1/apriltag/src/main/native/resources/edu/wpi/first/apriltag/2026-rebuilt-welded.json
+
+## Camera names and configuration
+
+Hardware hostnames and code now both use limelight-front, limelight-left, and
+limelight-right. Deploy this version after renaming the cameras. The dashboard
+must show Vision/ConfigurationID = three-ll4-named-2026-10-30 and
+Vision/ConfiguredCameraCount = 3. Limelights communicate over
+the robot's Ethernet/IP network, using NetworkTables to exchange data with the
+roboRIO; they are not motor-controller devices listed in Phoenix Tuner.
+
+For future hostname changes, connect the laptop to the robot network and use
+Limelight Hardware Manager to discover/open each camera, or use its IP address
+with port 5801 in a browser. Settings contains the hostname and team number.
+Give each camera a unique name, such as limelight-front, limelight-left, and
+limelight-right. Change the robot code's camera list, pose switch, logging-role
+mapping, and reset references in the same deployment. Renaming hardware alone
+would disconnect the old code from that camera's NetworkTables entries.
+
+Quick-start:
+https://docs.limelightvision.io/docs/docs-limelight/getting-started/limelight-4
+
+## Drawing the robot and diagnosing unreachable shots
+
+In AdvantageScope live, create a 2D Field tab. Drag the entire
+`AdvantageKit/RealOutputs/Swerve/Pose` structured value onto it, not its individual
+X/Y members. Alternatively use the `SmartDashboard/Swerve/Field` Field2d value.
+While the aimed shot is held, `Fuel/RobotPose`, `Fuel/HubPose`, and `Fuel/ReleasePose`
+are also structured Pose2d outputs suitable for overlaying on the same field.
+The hub is a reference marker, not another robot. These Fuel poses describe the
+last active shot execution; Swerve/Pose updates continuously.
+
+When Fuel/Status says "No reachable shot", read `Fuel/Trajectory/Reason`
+(or SmartDashboard/Fuel/TrajectoryReason). The trajectory folder records the
+range last solved, RPM search limits, launch angle/speed scale and progressive
+candidate counts: reached hub height, cleared near rim, landed in opening, and
+passed minimum entry angle. A zero at one stage identifies the first stage
+where all RPM candidates failed. No model trajectory is bypassed by these logs.
+Compare `Fuel/RangeMeters` with the actual distance to hub center, not its edge.
